laura_onboarding: hi all — new user reports our sample-app tests fail in CI (jest) with `SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module` when importing from '@redwood/inference'. Works locally with node 18. Any quick DX guidance we can add to the onboarding doc?

sam_sdk: that usually means Jest is running in CJS mode while package is published as ESM. Two quick options: 1) run Jest in ESM mode, or 2) require a transpiled CJS build. We should document both.

dmitry_tests: for Jest specifically, config snippets that helped us: 
```js
// jest.config.cjs (CJS runner) – tell jest to treat extensions as ESM
module.exports = {
  preset: 'ts-jest',
  testEnvironment: 'node',
  extensionsToTreatAsEsm: ['.ts', '.tsx', '.js'],
  transform: {
    '^.+\\.tsx?$': ['ts-jest', { useESM: true }]
  }
}
```

emma_devex: good call. Also add a short paragraph showing the minimal require fallback for folks who can't switch Jest yet: 

```js
// CommonJS fallback (works in older Node/jest configs)
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference').default || require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
```

laura_onboarding: should we mention package.json `type` and `exports` too? The user had an `exports` map in their app which made resolution fail in jest because jest resolves to the wrong entry.

sam_sdk: yes — add a short note: if your app's package.json sets "type":"module" your files are ESM by default. If you have an "exports" field, ensure the paths include a CJS fallback if you expect require() to work. Example: 

```json
// package.json exports example (simple)
{
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
    }
  }
}
```

dmitry_tests: another Jest gotcha: when using `ts-jest` + `useESM: true` you may need to run jest with `node --experimental-vm-modules` in older Node versions; note that in the doc as a troubleshooting step. Also mention `transformIgnorePatterns` if users' bundler libs are shipped as ESM and need to be transformed.

emma_devex: for TypeScript consumers, call out `import type` to avoid runtime import of types in ESM builds. Eg: 
```ts
import Redwood, { type ClientOptions } from '@redwood/inference';
// or separate types to be explicit
import type { ClientOptions } from '@redwood/inference';
```

laura_onboarding: can we include a tiny script in the sample-app that detects runtime module system and prints a hint? e.g. check for `process.env.NODE_OPTIONS` or `typeof module === 'undefined'`?

sam_sdk: yeah, simple snippet: 
```js
// debug/import-check.js
try {
  const mod = require('@redwood/inference');
  console.log('resolved via require, hasDefault=', !!mod.default);
} catch (e) {
  console.log('require failed, you may be in ESM-only runtime; try `import` or adjust jest config`');
}
```

dmitry_tests: also propose a CI matrix test: run the sample app tests under Node ESM, Node CJS (ts-jest), and Next.js edge build. Add a single assertion: `expect(typeof client.generate).toBe('function')` after importing to catch default-named mismatches.

emma_devex: I'll add a short FAQ entry: "Why does Jest/Bundler error on `import` from @redwood/inference?" with the above checklists and snippets. Will also link to the sample-app CI job.

docs-bot: Please link the PR to these files: /sample-app/README.md and /docs/sdk/js/imports.md. Also tag the CI job: `ci/sample-app/import-matrix`.

laura_onboarding: thanks all — will paste repro logs here if the user shares them. For now I'll open the docs PR with the Jest, ts-jest, CJS fallback, package.json exports, and the debug script. :tada:

sam_sdk: awesome. small wording nit: call the require fallback a "compat" snippet rather than recommended — prefer ESM for edge + modern Node.

emma_devex: noted. I'll label the examples: "Preferred (ESM)", "Compatibility (CJS/require)", and "Troubleshooting (Jest/ts-jest)". That should reduce confusion. :+1:
